{"conjecture": "P_y lies on a fixed parabola with focus F and vertex V. Its axis is the line j = FV, its tangent at the vertex is h1, and its directrix is e. Equivalently, FP_x^2 = FV · FP_y for every P_x on h1. The line P_xP_y (= m) is the tangent to this parabola at P_y.", "reason": "GF is a diameter, so j (through F, parallel to f) is the tangent to c at F. Use coordinates with F as the origin and j as the x-axis. Let the angle GAE be φ. The tangent g meets j at distance FV = v = r·cot(φ/2). The chord GE meets j at distance FD = 2r·cot(φ/2) = 2v. So the line e (x = 2v) is the reflection, through V, of the line perpendicular to j at F. Take P_x = (v, t) on h1 (the line x = v). Then K = (2v, 2t). The line m is perpendicular to FP_x at P_x. Intersecting m with the line y = 2t gives P_y = (v − t²/v, 2t). This satisfies x = v − y²/(4v), which is the parabola with vertex V, focus F and directrix e. Hence FP_y = P_yK = v + t²/v, and FP_x² = v² + t² = FV·FP_y. This is the classical fact that the foot of the perpendicular from the focus to a tangent lies on the tangent at the vertex. Numerical check: FP_x² ≈ 26.200 and FV·FP_y ≈ 2.3094 × 11.345 ≈ 26.200.", "closes_goal": true}
```
